        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Jackery Size Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing a Jackery comes down to two numbers: how many watts your devices draw at the same time, which sets the output you need, and how many watt-hours they use over the period you care about, which sets the capacity. Get the first wrong and the unit shuts off the moment you plug in a kettle. Get the second wrong and it runs out at 2 a.m. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">power station sizing guide</a> walks through the full calculation, but the short version for Jackery buyers is below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Devices and day trips: Explorer 300 Plus</h3>

          <p>
            If the job is phones, laptops, cameras, drone batteries and a light or two, 288Wh is enough for a day and 8.3 pounds is easy to carry. It is also a reasonable choice for one CPAP night if you turn off the heated humidifier, which can triple the machine's draw. Our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">running a CPAP on a power station</a> has the numbers by machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weekend camping and vans: Explorer 500 v2</h3>

          <p>
            Two nights of CPAP, a 12V cooler for most of a day, lights, fans and device charging for a family. The 500W inverter is the limit, not the battery; anything that heats will trip it. For a car-camping weekend, this is the size that covers almost everything without becoming a chore to carry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outages and serious camping: Explorer 1000 v2 or 1000 Plus</h3>

          <p>
            This is where a Jackery starts acting as home backup. Around 1kWh keeps a full-size fridge cold through an evening outage, or a fridge plus router and lights through a short one. Our explainer on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">running a refrigerator on a power station</a> covers compressor startup surge, which both of these handle comfortably. Choose the 1000 v2 for weight and the 1000 Plus for output and expansion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Multi-day outages and RVs: 2000 v2 or HomePower 2000 Plus v2</h3>

          <p>
            At 2kWh you can run a refrigerator for most of a day and keep internet and lights going alongside it, or power an RV's essentials for a long weekend. If you need more than one day off-grid, the HomePower 2000 Plus v2's expansion and 800W solar input make it the better long-term buy. Only choose the Explorer 5000 Plus if you have 240V loads to run, because no smaller Jackery can start a 240V well pump.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="model-names" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Explorer, Plus and HomePower: Decoding Jackery's Names
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Number in the Name Tells You</h3>

          <p>
            Jackery's naming has become confusing enough that buyers regularly end up with the wrong unit, so it is worth decoding. The number in the name roughly tracks capacity in watt-hours: a 1000 holds about 1kWh, a 2000 about 2kWh, a 5000 about 5kWh. It is approximate, not exact, which is why the Explorer 1000 v2 holds 1,070Wh and the 1000 Plus holds 1,264Wh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The v2 Suffix</h3>

          <p>
            A v2 suffix marks the current generation of Jackery's fixed-capacity Explorer models. The v2 units moved to LiFePO4 cells, shed weight and added app-controlled emergency charging. They do not expand. A Plus suffix, as in Explorer 1000 Plus or Explorer 5000 Plus, means the unit takes Jackery's expansion batteries and usually has more output and solar input than the matching v2. The exception is the Explorer 300 Plus, which is simply the LiFePO4 successor to the original Explorer 300 and does not expand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HomePower and E-Prefixed Model Codes</h3>

          <p>
            HomePower is the newest family. The HomePower 1000 Plus v2 and HomePower 2000 Plus v2 are expandable, backup-focused units with faster UPS switchover and higher cycle ratings, and the 2000 Plus v2 replaces the heavy Explorer 2000 Plus that many older reviews still recommend. If you see the Explorer 2000 Plus at a steep discount, it remains a capable 2kWh, 3,000W expandable unit, but at 61.5 pounds it is a lot of box to move.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, some retailers and Jackery's own site use E-prefixed model codes, such as E1000 Plus, in product listings. Those are the same Explorer units with a different label, not separate products. Match the capacity, output and chemistry on the spec sheet rather than trusting the name alone, especially when shopping for clearance or refurbished units.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Original Pre-v2 Explorers</h3>

          <p>
            The original, pre-v2 Explorer 300, 500, 1000 and 1500 used NMC lithium-ion cells rated for around 500 cycles to 80 percent. They are still sold used and occasionally new on clearance, often under the exact same names minus the suffix. Our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion explainer</a> covers why that difference matters if you plan to keep the unit for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar-panels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. SolarSaga Panels and Solar Charging
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The SolarSaga Range</h3>

          <p>
            Jackery's SolarSaga panels come in several sizes, from the 40W Mini that suits the Explorer 300 Plus through the popular SolarSaga 100W and SolarSaga 200W folding panels, up to larger panels intended for the HomePower and 5000 Plus systems. They fold like a briefcase, stand on built-in kickstands and connect directly to Jackery's solar inputs without adapters, which is the main reason to buy matched panels rather than third-party ones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Solar Input Ceiling</h3>

          <p>
            The number to match is the power station's solar input ceiling. The Explorer 300 Plus takes 100W, the 500 v2 takes 200W, the 1000 v2 and 2000 v2 take 400W, and the 1000 Plus and HomePower 2000 Plus v2 take 800W. Connecting more panel wattage than the ceiling does not charge faster; the unit simply limits what it draws. Connecting much less leaves you waiting. As a starting point, aim for panel wattage close to the input ceiling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Real Sun Delivers Less Than the Rating</h3>

          <p>
            Then adjust your expectations. A panel's rated wattage is measured in lab conditions, and in real sun most folding panels deliver 60 to 80 percent of their rating, less in morning and evening light, on hot days or when not angled at the sun. Two SolarSaga 200W panels feeding an Explorer 1000 v2 typically deliver something like 250 to 320W at midday, which refills about 900Wh of usable capacity in three to four hours of strong sun and closer to a full day in mixed weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Third-Party Panels and the SolarSaga Premium</h3>

          <p>
            If you already own panels, check two things before buying a Jackery: the panel's open-circuit voltage must fall inside the station's input window, which runs from about 12 to 60 volts on the Explorer v2 models, and you may need an adapter cable for Jackery's connector. Our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels guide</a> covers panels that work well with Jackery stations, and our walkthrough on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a power station with solar</a> explains series and parallel wiring when you connect more than one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Is SolarSaga worth the premium over generic folding panels? For most buyers it is a reasonable choice in a bundle, where the price gap often shrinks, and a harder sell bought separately. Third-party panels of the same wattage usually cost less and charge the station just as well, provided the voltage and connector match.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-ecoflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Jackery vs EcoFlow: Which Brand Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            EcoFlow is the brand most Jackery shoppers also consider, and the honest answer is that each wins different categories. Our full <a href="/jackery-vs-ecoflow/">Jackery vs EcoFlow comparison</a> goes model by model; here is the short version for someone deciding which Jackery to buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where EcoFlow Wins</h3>

          <p>
            EcoFlow generally puts more inverter output, faster wall charging and more solar input into each size class below the whole-home tier. At 1kWh, the DELTA 3 Plus offers 1,800W of output against the Explorer 1000 v2's 1,500W, refills from the wall in under an hour in standard mode, accepts 1,000W of solar against 400W, and expands to 5kWh. It also has six AC outlets to Jackery's three and a faster UPS switchover. For a unit that will sit in a utility room as backup, those are real advantages.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Jackery Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Jackery wins on weight and simplicity. The Explorer 1000 v2 is nearly four pounds lighter than the DELTA 3 Plus, and the Explorer 2000 v2 is about five pounds lighter than the DELTA 3 Max. Its controls and app are simpler, which matters when someone who has not touched the unit in months needs to operate it in the dark. And at the very top end, the Explorer 5000 Plus out-powers EcoFlow's DELTA Pro 3, with 7,200W against 4,000W.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our Recommendation</h3>

          <p>
            Our recommendation: if your priority is camping, RV use or a first power station that anyone in the family can carry and operate, buy a Jackery. If it is home backup, particularly with solar, EcoFlow's DELTA 3 Plus is the more capable machine at the same size, which is why it leads our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power station guide</a> overall while the Explorer 1000 v2 is our value pick. If you are also weighing Bluetti, our <a href="/bluetti-vs-ecoflow/">Bluetti vs EcoFlow comparison</a> fills in the third brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-app" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Warranty, App and Charging Modes
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warranty Terms</h3>

          <p>
            Jackery lists most current models with a 3 plus 2 year warranty: three years standard, plus two more when the unit is bought directly from Jackery or an authorized seller. The Explorer 5000 Plus carries a five-year warranty. Buying from a third-party marketplace seller who is not authorized can cost you the extra two years, so check the seller before you check out, register the unit the day it arrives and keep the receipt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The App and Emergency Charging</h3>

          <p>
            The Jackery app connects over Wi-Fi or Bluetooth on current models and does three useful things. It shows input and output in real time, which is the easiest way to learn what your appliances actually draw. It lets you switch outlets and set charge limits remotely. And on the Explorer v2 models it unlocks emergency charging, which cuts wall charging from about 1.6 hours to roughly one hour on the 1000 v2. Without the app you get the slower standard rate, so install it before you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Emergency mode is meant for exactly what it sounds like, a fast top-up before a storm or between outages. Jackery's standard charging is gentler on the battery and quieter, so leave the unit on standard for routine charging. The fans become clearly audible during fast charging or heavy loads on any Jackery, which is worth knowing if the unit lives in a bedroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage and UPS Use</h3>

          <p>
            For long-term storage, keep the unit between about 50 and 80 percent, store it somewhere cool and dry, and top it up every three to six months. LiFePO4 holds charge well, but no battery likes sitting full in a hot garage. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/">how long portable power stations last</a> explains what actually wears a battery out and how to slow it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several Jackery models also work as an uninterruptible power supply, keeping a router, modem or desktop running through a blackout. The Explorer 1000 v2 lists a switchover under 20 ms, the HomePower 1000 Plus v2 under 10 ms, and the 5000 Plus offers a 0 ms online mode. Most network gear rides through 20 ms without noticing, but some older desktop power supplies can reset, so choose a faster unit if a home server or workstation is the reason you are buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Jackery Models to Skip
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Original NMC Explorers on Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            Not every Jackery still on sale is worth buying. The most common mistake we see is a buyer picking up an original Explorer 1000 or Explorer 1500 on clearance because the price looks attractive next to the v2 models. Those units use NMC lithium-ion cells rated for roughly 500 cycles, slower charging and lower output. Even at a deep discount they are worse long-term value than a current LiFePO4 model, and many are already several years into their shelf life before they reach you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The original Explorer 300 and Explorer 500 fall into the same trap. They are fine for occasional device charging, but the Explorer 300 Plus and Explorer 500 v2 cost little more, last many times longer in cycle terms and charge faster. Unless an older unit is nearly free, skip it. When buying refurbished, confirm the chemistry on the spec sheet rather than relying on the product name.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavy Legacy Models</h3>

          <p>
            We would also be cautious about the heaviest legacy models where a lighter successor exists. The Explorer 2000 Plus remains capable, but at 61.5 pounds against roughly 41 for the HomePower 2000 Plus v2, it is only worth buying at a clear discount, and only if the unit will rarely move. Its expansion batteries are not interchangeable with the HomePower line, so check which packs you can still buy before committing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Big Bundles With Small Panels</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, be skeptical of large bundles that pair a big battery with a single small panel. A 2kWh unit with one 100W panel will not keep up with a refrigerator from solar alone, no matter what the box art suggests. If solar charging matters to you, buy panel wattage close to the station's input limit, or buy the bare unit and choose panels yourself. And if what you really need is whole-home coverage, compare the Explorer 5000 Plus against the systems in our <a href="/best-home-battery-backup-system/">home battery backup guide</a> before committing, because permanently installed batteries can be better value at that scale.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best Jackery power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Explorer 1000 v2. It holds 1,070Wh, outputs 1,500W, weighs 23.8 pounds and charges in about an hour in emergency mode, which covers camping and short outages well. For dedicated home backup, the HomePower 2000 Plus v2 is the better choice thanks to 2,400W of output, 800W solar input and expansion to about 12kWh.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Jackery solar generator the same as a Jackery power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, apart from the panels. A Jackery Solar Generator is an Explorer or HomePower power station bundled with one or more SolarSaga folding solar panels. The battery, inverter and outlets are identical to the bare unit. There is no gas engine involved, so it is silent, produces no fumes and can be used indoors safely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a Jackery run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use about 85 percent of rated capacity divided by your fridge's average draw. A modern full-size fridge averages around 120W, so the Explorer 1000 v2 runs it about seven and a half hours, the 2000 v2 or HomePower 2000 Plus v2 about 14 hours, and the Explorer 5000 Plus well over a day. Older or larger fridges draw more and run shorter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a Jackery power station run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only the Explorer 5000 Plus comes close. Its 7,200W 120/240V output can run a well pump, central air handler and essential circuits through a transfer switch installed by an electrician. Smaller Jackery models run selected appliances on extension cords, such as a fridge, furnace blower, router and lights, but not 240V loads or a whole home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Jackery better than EcoFlow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the job. Jackery units are lighter and simpler to use, which makes them better for camping, RVs and first-time buyers. EcoFlow generally offers more output, faster wall charging and higher solar input at each size, which suits home backup. At the whole-home tier, Jackery's Explorer 5000 Plus out-powers EcoFlow's DELTA Pro 3 on output.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do Jackery power stations last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Current Jackery models use LiFePO4 cells rated for 3,000 to 6,000-plus cycles, depending on the model. The Explorer 1000 v2 is rated for 4,000 cycles to 70 percent capacity, which is more than ten years of daily full cycles. Most owners will replace the unit for other reasons long before the battery wears out, provided it is stored sensibly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use non-Jackery solar panels with a Jackery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as the panel's voltage fits the station's solar input window and you have the right connector. Explorer v2 models accept roughly 12 to 60 volts. Many third-party panels need an adapter cable for Jackery's input. Stay within the station's wattage limit too, since extra panel wattage beyond it will not charge any faster.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by the number in the name</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The number in a Jackery's name is only a rough guide to capacity, and it says nothing about output. The Explorer 1000 v2 and 1000 Plus both carry 1000 in the name, yet one outputs 1,500W and the other 2,000W. Read the inverter rating and the watt-hours on the spec sheet before you buy, not the badge on the case.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the solar input ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buyers often connect three or four panels to an Explorer 1000 v2 expecting a fast refill, then wonder why it charges no faster than with two. The 1000 v2 and 2000 v2 cap solar at 400W. Extra panels beyond that are wasted money. If solar matters, buy a model with an 800W input instead of adding panels.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an old NMC Explorer on clearance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Original Explorer 300, 500, 1000 and 1500 units use NMC cells rated for around 500 cycles. A discount of a hundred dollars or so rarely makes up for losing thousands of cycles of life and slower charging. Unless the unit is close to free, a current LiFePO4 v2 or Plus model is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the warranty registration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery's extra two warranty years depend on buying from Jackery or an authorized seller, and the purchase details matter if you ever make a claim. Register the unit in the app or on Jackery's site the day it arrives and keep the receipt. An unregistered marketplace purchase can leave you with only the three-year standard term.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a 1kWh unit to run a house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1kWh Jackery keeps a fridge, router and lights going through an evening outage. It will not run central air, an electric water heater or a 240V well pump, and it will not last a full day with a fridge alone. For multi-day outages, step up to the HomePower 2000 Plus v2 with expansion or the Explorer 5000 Plus.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

